Q: Correct syntax for function template with an even number of `int` arguments? A: <p>I think <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/2684539/jarod42">Jarod42</a>'s answer is probably what you want. Mostly because pow cannot be calculated at compile time and so there is not much to be gained here.</p>
<p>For completeness though, I thought I would add an example that shows an example of what you originally asked:</p>
<p><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79872247/correct-syntax-for-function-template-with-an-even-number-of-int-arguments">Correct syntax for function template with an even number of `int` arguments?</a><br />
Here is a pattern I sometimes use. It is most useful for older language standards, as <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/constexpr.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">constexpr</a> made most of these techniques redundant. However, there are times when tricks like this still make sense.<br />
In the example, I show a template &quot;div_helper&quot; that takes two ints at a time and defines a function that returns X/Y. It then builds (recursively) a function for each pair.</p>
<p>The second (div_helper2) example uses a structure to hold pairs of values, which is easier to maintain.<br />
I tested this in Visual Studio in conformant C++14 mode.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;

// div_helper: An example of a template that consumes integer parameters in pairs
template&lt;int ...&gt; struct div_helper;
template&lt;int X, int Y&gt; struct div_helper&lt;X,Y&gt; { static double value() { return static_cast&lt;double&gt;(X)/static_cast&lt;double&gt;(Y); } };
// recursion step
template&lt;int X, int Y, int ... Xs&gt; struct div_helper&lt;X, Y, Xs...&gt; { static double value() { return div_helper&lt;X,Y&gt;::value() + div_helper&lt;Xs...&gt;::value(); } };


// div_helper2: An example of a template that declares a structure to hold pairs
template&lt;int X, int Y&gt; struct div_pair {
    static int get_X() { return X; }
    static int get_Y() { return Y; }
};
template&lt;class...&gt; struct div_helper2;
template&lt;class P&gt; struct div_helper2&lt;P&gt; { static double value() { return static_cast&lt;double&gt;(P::get_X()) / static_cast&lt;double&gt;(P::get_Y()); } };
template&lt;class P, class...Ps&gt; struct div_helper2&lt;P,Ps...&gt; { static double value() { return div_helper2&lt;P&gt;::value() + div_helper2&lt;Ps...&gt;::value(); } };


int main()
{
    // Consuming two at a time works, but can get hard to maintain
    std::cout &lt;&lt; div_helper&lt;2,3,3,4&gt;::value() &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;
    std::cout &lt;&lt; div_helper&lt;1, 2&gt;::value() &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;
      //std::cout &lt;&lt; div_helper&lt;1&gt;::value() &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;; does not compile, but confusing 
    // Consuming structures is more verbose, but more readable.
    std::cout &lt;&lt; div_helper2&lt;div_pair&lt;1, 2&gt;, div_pair&lt;3, 4&gt;&gt;::value() &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;
}
</code></pre>
